<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lusatia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lusatia is a historical region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Oder, divided into Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a> and today split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It is the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, a Slavic people. Both parts belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Lands of the Bohemian Crown</a> from the fourteenth century until 1635, when they passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>; in 1815 most of the region went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lusatia.jpg" alt="Lusatia" width="320" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_LUS2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bohemian estates elected him king in October 1526, bringing Bohemia, Moravia, Silesia and the two <b>Lusatias</b> under Habsburg rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1635 the <b>Lusatias</b> went to Saxony, and in 1742 Prussia took most of Silesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The German colonisation of the lands east of the Elbe, in which the Ascanian margraves took a leading part, reshaped the ethnic and linguistic map of central Europe, though the Slavic Sorbs of <b>Lusatia</b> survive as a minority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Henry II and Duke Bolesław I the Brave of Poland concluded the Peace of Bautzen, ending fifteen years of intermittent war over <b>Lusatia</b>, Meissen and Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-30">30 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Boleslaw I fought the emperor Henry II for years, gaining <b>Lusatia</b> at the Peace of Bautzen in 1018, and the same year briefly took Kiev.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-poland">Duchy of Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1656, Christian I received Merseburg, the secularised bishopric and its district, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg, which also held parts of <b>Lusatia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-merseburg">Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg</a></li></ul></section>
